<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0f2f7;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Athletes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond appropriately to spoken questions in familiar settings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend multi-step instructions related to presentations about famous Panamanians (e.g., First, research the achievements of a famous Panamanian. Next, create a slide with their most important accomplishment. Finally, rehearse your presentation.")<br>Can respond to spoken questions about famous Panamanians (e.g., "Who is the most famous athlete?")<br>Can summarize information from discussions on public figures and share key takeaways (e.g., "She has won many awards, including Athlete of the Year.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon, Roberto Durán). The teacher asks simple listening questions: "Who is this person?", "Is he/she famous?", "What sport does he/she play?". Students listen and respond orally (e.g., "Mariano Rivera.", "Yes, he is famous.", "He plays baseball."). This introduces the theme "My Favorite Athletes!" and activates prior knowledge about sports and famous Panamanians.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new vocabulary using flashcards with pictures of athletes performing actions. For example, pointing to a picture of an athlete winning, the teacher says: "He is an <b>athlete</b>. He likes to <b>compete</b>. He wants to <b>win</b> an <b>award</b>. He is <b>training</b> for a new tournament." The teacher clearly models pronunciation of new words (e.g., "athlete," "win," "award," "competing") and specifically practices -ing verb endings ("watching," "training") and -ed in past tense ("performed," "won").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary through targeted listening questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is an <b>athlete</b> someone who plays sports or someone who sings?" (Expected answer: "Someone who plays sports.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you <b>win</b> a competition, do you get first place or last place?" (Expected answer: "First place.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a great <b>achievement</b>? Is it something easy or something difficult and important you do?" (Expected answer: "Something difficult and important.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Famous Panamanians")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of short descriptive sentences about three different famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon, Roberto Durán), focusing on their achievements and characteristics. Each description uses the target vocabulary and grammar structures (Present Continuous for future events, Past Simple for achievements, Comparatives/Superlatives).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist."). Pragmatic: Describing achievements: "Mariano Rivera won many awards."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and raise their hand when they hear the name of an athlete they recognize. The teacher asks: "Who did you hear about?" (Expected answers: "Roberto Durán," "Mariano Rivera," "Atheyna Bylon.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio again, pausing after each description. Students listen carefully and decide if statements read by the teacher are True or False based on the audio. For example: "Mariano Rivera won many football awards." (False) "Atheyna Bylon is training for a new competition." (True) "Roberto Durán is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Listening):</b> Students listen to an instruction like: "Listen for the sport each athlete plays." Then, the audio plays, and students match the athlete's name to their sport from a list provided by the teacher on the board or a worksheet.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening: Comprehending multi-step instructions and specific information. Grammar: Present continuous for future events (e.g., "She is training for a new tournament."), Comparatives and superlatives (e.g., "Roberto Durán is better known than other boxers."), Past simple for achievements (e.g., "Atheyna Bylon won a medal."). Vocabulary: athlete, achievement, award, win, achieve, inspire, train, compete, famous, popular, more, best. Pronunciation: -ing verb endings and -ed in past tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher plays short audio clips describing an athlete, but with a key vocabulary word or grammar structure missing. Students listen and complete sentences on a worksheet. For example: "Mariano Rivera _____ (win) many awards." (Students write 'won'). "Atheyna Bylon _____ (train) for the Olympics next year." (Students write 'is training'). "He is a very _____ (fame) athlete." (Students write 'famous').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Listen and Identify):</b> The teacher gives students a list of sentences. The teacher then reads aloud a sentence, and students must listen and identify if the sentence describes an 'achievement' or a 'future event'. Students mark 'A' for achievement or 'F' for future event on their papers. Example sentences: "She won a gold medal." (A) "He is competing next month." (F) "She achieved her goal." (A)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listen and Order):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher reads a set of three sentences describing an athlete's journey out of order (e.g., "He won a championship in 2000." "He started playing baseball at 5." "He is now coaching a youth team."). Students listen to the sentences twice and then work together to order them chronologically on their worksheet, checking with their partner. This involves listening for time markers and sequencing.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Famous Panamanians")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Listening comprehension task leading to a short spoken summary and direct answers to questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Theme: "My Favorite Athletes!" and Scenario: "Famous Panamanians":</b> Students will listen to a short audio profile (1-2 minutes) of a *new* famous Panamanian athlete (e.g., Irving Saladino or Alonso Edward) whose information was not presented before. After listening, they will orally answer 2-3 spoken questions from the teacher about the athlete's achievements or future plans. For example, after the audio, the teacher asks: "What is his most important <b>achievement</b>?" or "Is he still <b>competing</b>?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in responding to spoken questions about what they heard, demonstrating comprehension of the multi-step instructions (listen, then answer), and using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., past simple for achievements, present continuous for future actions).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(7-8 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and informal peer assessment during pair activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short audio clip (or reads aloud) 3-4 statements about various famous Panamanians (not only athletes) using the target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and then verbally state if each statement is True or False, or respond to a direct comprehension question. For example, after hearing "Sech is more popular than other singers," the teacher asks: "Is Sech a famous athlete?" (Expected answer: "No, he is a famous singer."). After hearing "She has won many awards, including Athlete of the Year," the teacher asks: "What has she won?" (Expected answer: "Many awards, including Athlete of the Year.")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation when responding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., athlete, achievement, award, win) in responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated comprehension of spoken questions and statements (True/False or direct answer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow multi-step spoken instructions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an oral exit ticket by answering:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I understand sentences about famous Panamanian athletes?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy/difficult for me to understand when listening today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I learn today about famous athletes and their achievements?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What helped me understand the audio clips better?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to comprehend basic information and respond to simple questions, especially after repeated listening. The pronunciation practice for -ing and -ed endings was beneficial. Some students struggled with multi-step instructions, requiring an extra pause or rephrasing. I need to continue scaffolding listening activities and provide more visuals in the next lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter the same vocabulary and grammar structures in a written context, focusing on reading comprehension about famous Panamanians. This listening foundation will support their understanding of the texts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3-5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>